priya: Quick note from debugging the Kotlin SDK parity issue: multiple teams hit duplicate POSTs when Authenticator does a blocking refresh. Repro on emulator API 33/34.
omar: yeah saw that. What's your root cause?
priya: two things combined: Authenticator doing a synchronous refresh on the same OkHttpClient (runBlocking or sync Retrofit call) + non-repeatable RequestBody. Dispatcher gets starved and requests are retried/replayed.
ben: classic. Recommended pattern: single-flight token refresh (so only one refresh happens) + dedicated client for refresh if you must do a sync call.
priya: here's the small single-flight example we added to the sample app (Kotlin):
```kotlin
private val refreshLock = Mutex()
private var tokenDeferred: Deferred<String?>? = null
suspend fun singleFlightRefresh(): String? {
  refreshLock.withLock {
    if (tokenDeferred == null) {
      tokenDeferred = CoroutineScope(Dispatchers.IO).async { authApi.refreshToken() }
    }
    return try {
      tokenDeferred!!.await()
    } finally {
      tokenDeferred = null
    }
  }
}
```
ben: usage: in an Interceptor you can call singleFlightRefresh() from a coroutine path and retry the request once token is updated. Avoid calling network synchronously inside OkHttp Authenticator.
omar: if you can't make the authenticator async, the simpler fix is to create a tiny dedicated OkHttpClient for refresh and inject it into the auth service: `Retrofit.Builder().client(authClient).build()` where authClient has its own Dispatcher. That solved parity bugs for our java vs kotlin samples.
priya: also add these practical mitigations to devex docs: 1) avoid blocking network calls on same client/dispatcher in Authenticator; 2) make request bodies repeatable for mutating endpoints or use idempotency-key headers; 3) consider disabling `retryOnConnectionFailure` for clients that send non-repeatable streaming bodies.
ben: small snippet to disable retries: `OkHttpClient.Builder().retryOnConnectionFailure(false).build()`
priya: filed a tiny sample + test in kotlin-sdk-repros showing single-flight vs dedicated-client approaches: https://github.com/redwood-inference/kotlin-sdk-repros/pull/130
